1
00:00:00,180 --> 00:00:08,640
So, uh, welcome to today's deep dive. We're looking at digital transformation today, specifically this massive shift from, you know, just

2
00:00:09,020 --> 00:00:15,480
putting paper forms online to fundamentally redesigning how complex organizations actually operate.

3
00:00:15,660 --> 00:00:19,779
Right. Because, I mean, for a long time, the whole mission was just digitizing the old stuff.

4
00:00:19,900 --> 00:00:27,640
Yeah, exactly. Like turning a physical queue into an online appointment or, uh, taking a paper certificate and just making it a PDF.

5
00:00:28,120 --> 00:00:31,000
It's basically putting a digital skin on an analog skeleton.

6
00:00:31,030 --> 00:00:33,720
That is a really great way to put it, a digital skin.

7
00:00:33,760 --> 00:00:39,820
Yeah. But, um, while that makes things faster for you and your team, it doesn't change the underlying operating model at all.

8
00:00:39,880 --> 00:00:48,620
Which brings us to the big question for you all listening: If that digital skin makes things faster, why is that approach just no longer enough?

9
00:00:48,900 --> 00:00:58,740
Well, because the primary work today isn't just about moving forms to a website, it's about entirely redesigning how people are served, and that requires changing the whole underlying operating

10
00:00:58,820 --> 00:00:58,960
model.

11
00:00:59,000 --> 00:01:00,940
Right. Which means connecting a lot of different dots.

12
00:01:01,320 --> 00:01:05,740
Exactly. And our sources give us three really concrete examples of this new work.

13
00:01:06,540 --> 00:01:07,460
So number one

14
00:01:08,240 --> 00:01:10,420
is the, uh, one farmer register.

15
00:01:10,620 --> 00:01:13,660
Oh, so instead of a bunch of separate agricultural databases.

16
00:01:13,740 --> 00:01:21,380
Yes, exactly. A single system shared seamlessly by, say, agriculture ministries, cooperative banks, and climate resilience programs.

17
00:01:21,600 --> 00:01:24,740
Wow. Okay. That is a massive integration.

18
00:01:24,800 --> 00:01:27,760
It is. And number two is one national identity.

19
00:01:27,840 --> 00:01:33,140
Right. So allowing individuals to prove who they are at any service without lugging around physical paper.

20
00:01:33,200 --> 00:01:40,880
Exactly. And then the third one is one learner record, so a continuous file that follows a student from primary school all the way through university.

21
00:01:41,160 --> 00:01:45,490
That makes so much sense. But, I mean, none of those examples are just putting existing forms online.

22
00:01:45,700 --> 00:01:48,960
No, not at all. They require entirely different entities to integrate.

23
00:01:49,020 --> 00:01:53,210
So to build these unified systems, the old dynamic just doesn't work anymore, does it?

24
00:01:53,240 --> 00:02:00,040
The days of business leaders just dictating the what and IT engineers quietly delivering the how, that's dead.

25
00:02:00,280 --> 00:02:02,600
Completely dead. They have to make decisions together now.

26
00:02:02,660 --> 00:02:04,400
Okay, but let me push back on that a bit.

27
00:02:04,460 --> 00:02:12,860
If both the business side and the IT side actually want these unified systems, why is getting in a room and deciding together so incredibly difficult?

28
00:02:12,940 --> 00:02:13,360
Honestly,

29
00:02:14,080 --> 00:02:18,840
it's a hidden language barrier. They sit in the exact same meeting and completely miss each other.

30
00:02:18,880 --> 00:02:21,140
Wait, really? How does that happen?

31
00:02:21,160 --> 00:02:26,970
Well, think about it. Policy heads, they talk about citizen services, broad goals, and social impact, right?

32
00:02:27,040 --> 00:02:27,620
Yeah, of course.

33
00:02:27,760 --> 00:02:32,109
But the IT architects are talking about APIs, systems, and data structures.

34
00:02:32,620 --> 00:02:35,340
Ah. So they literally aren't speaking the same language.

35
00:02:35,460 --> 00:02:38,510
Exactly. And because they lack a shared language, decisions stall.

36
00:02:39,180 --> 00:02:43,710
And what happens? Institutions just regress right back to the old work of putting paper online.

37
00:02:43,920 --> 00:02:47,120
Because it's easier. So what is the structural solution to this?

38
00:02:47,140 --> 00:02:48,400
We need a missing link here.

39
00:02:48,420 --> 00:02:55,240
The solution is a national enterprise architecture, or EA for short, and it's anchored on frameworks like PAERA.

40
00:02:55,340 --> 00:03:01,000
Right. PAERA, the Public Administration Ecosystem Reference Architecture.

41
00:03:01,329 --> 00:03:01,780
You got it.

42
00:03:02,500 --> 00:03:07,630
And an EA provides three essential elements to create that shared language we were just talking about.

43
00:03:08,180 --> 00:03:10,040
First, it gives you the agreed picture.

44
00:03:10,080 --> 00:03:12,609
Like a visual model they can actually point to in a meeting.

45
00:03:12,760 --> 00:03:13,720
Exactly, yes. Okay.

46
00:03:13,740 --> 00:03:14,350
Something physical.

47
00:03:15,000 --> 00:03:17,329
Second, it provides plain words for basic terms.

48
00:03:17,900 --> 00:03:25,360
So ensuring terms like service, capability, or data domain mean the exact same thing to policymakers and software engineers.

49
00:03:25,620 --> 00:03:28,540
That seems so basic, but I bet it is constantly overlooked.

50
00:03:28,600 --> 00:03:31,220
Oh, absolutely. And third is the EA board.

51
00:03:31,260 --> 00:03:35,680
Which is a regular structured forum designed specifically for joint decision-making, right?

52
00:03:35,840 --> 00:03:41,240
Exactly. In the era of digitizing paper, an EA was, you know, maybe just a useful tool.

53
00:03:41,320 --> 00:03:45,940
But in this new era of entirely redesigning systems, it's absolutely necessary.

54
00:03:46,040 --> 00:03:46,900
Hundred percent. Yeah.

55
00:03:46,960 --> 00:03:51,299
You simply cannot change your operating model until you establish that shared language first.

56
00:03:51,800 --> 00:03:58,960
So to sum it up for everyone listening, true digital transformation really isn't just an IT issue, it's a communication challenge.

57
00:03:59,060 --> 00:04:06,380
Yes. And it requires a framework like EA to finally bridge that gap between policy vision and technical reality.

58
00:04:06,440 --> 00:04:09,130
Which leaves us with a really fascinating thought to end on:

59
00:04:10,000 --> 00:04:19,899
If an architectural framework can successfully force engineers and policymakers to finally speak the same language, could applying these exact same shared language frameworks be

60
00:04:19,940 --> 00:04:24,180
the secret to breaking down toxic silos in completely different fields?

61
00:04:24,320 --> 00:04:27,600
Oh, like global healthcare or multinational corporate finance.

62
00:04:27,640 --> 00:04:30,310
Exactly. If it works here, it could work anywhere.

63
00:04:30,340 --> 00:04:31,760
Definitely something for you to think about.
